.SH NAME
delta \- make a delta (change) to an SCCS file
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBdelta \fR[\fIoptions\fR ] \fIfiles\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Delta
is used to permanently introduce into
the named SCCS file changes that were made to the file retrieved by
.BR get (1)
(called the g-file: generated file).
.PP
.B Delta
makes a change to each named SCCS file.
If a directory is named,
.B delta
behaves as though each file in the directory were
a named argument.
However, non-SCCS files
(last component of the pathname does not begin with \fBs.\fR)
and unreadable files
are silently ignored.
If \fB\-\fR is given as an argument, standard input is read
(see \fBWARNINGS\fR).
Each line of the standard input is taken to be the name of an SCCS file
to be processed.
.PP
.B Delta
may issue prompts on the standard output depending upon
certain keyletters specified and flags
that may be
present in the SCCS file
(see \fBadmin\fR(1) and the
.B \-m
and
.B \-y
keyletters below).
.PP
Keyletter arguments
apply independently
to each named file.
.TP
\fB-r\fISID\fR
Uniquely
identifies which delta is to be made to the SCCS file.
The use of this keyletter is necessary only if two or more
outstanding
.BR get s
.RB ( "get \-e" )
requests-for-editing against the same SCCS file were entered by the same person
(login name).
The SID value specified with the
.B \-r
keyletter can be either the SID specified on the
.B get
command line or the SID to be created as reported by the
.B get
command.
A diagnostic results if the specified SID is ambiguous, or
if it is necessary but omitted on the command line.
.TP
\fB-g\fIlist\fR
Specifies
a \fIlist\fR
(see \fBget\fR(1)
for the definition of \fIlist\fR)
of deltas which are to be ignored
when the file is accessed at the change level (SID)
created by this delta. If the list has more than one member, enclose the
list in double quotes.
.TP
\fB-m[\fIMRlist]\fR
If
the SCCS file has the
.B v
flag set
(see
.BR admin (1))
then a Modification Request (MR) number must be
supplied as the reason for creating the new delta.
If more than one MR number is given, the list must be enclosed in
double quotes. 
.sp
If
.B \-m
is not used and the standard input is a terminal, the prompt
.B MRs?
is issued on the standard output before the standard input
is read; if the standard input is not a terminal, no prompt is issued.
The
.B MRs?
prompt always precedes the
.B comments?
prompt
(see
.B \-y
keyletter).
.sp
MRs entered interactively in this manner are separated by
blanks and/or tab characters.
An unescaped new-line character terminates the MR list.
.sp
Note that if the
.B v
flag has a value
it is interpreted as the name of a program 
(or shell procedure) which will validate
the correctness of the MR numbers.
If a non-zero exit status is returned 
from the MR number validation program,
.B delta
terminates
(it is assumed that the MR numbers were not all valid).
.TP
\fB-n\fR
Specifies retention of the edited
g-file normally removed at completion of delta processing.
.TP
\fB-p\fR
Causes
.B delta
to print (on the standard output) the SCCS file
differences before and after the delta is applied
in a
.BR diff (1)
format.
.TP
\fB-s\fR
Suppresses
issuance, on the standard output, of the created delta's SID,
as well as the number of lines inserted, deleted and unchanged in the
SCCS file.
.TP
\fB-y[\fIcomment\fR]
Arbitrary text
used to describe the reason for making the delta.
If the comment is comprised of more than one word, enclose the comment in
double quotes.
A null string is considered a valid \fIcomment\fR.
.sp
If
.B \-y
is not specified and the standard input is a terminal, the prompt
.B comments?
is issued on the standard output before the standard
input is read. If the standard input is not a terminal, no
prompt is issued.
An unescaped new-line character terminates the comment text.
.SH FILES
All files of the form
.IR ?- file
are explained in the
\fISource Code Control System User's Guide\fR.
The naming convention for these files is also described there.
.TP
d-file
Created during the execution of
.BR delta ;
removed after completion of
.BR delta .
.bp
.TP
g-file
Existed
before the execution of
.BR delta ;
removed after completion of
.BR delta .
.TP
p-file
Existed
before the execution of
.BR delta ;
may exist after completion of
.BR delta .
.TP
q-file
Created during the execution of
.BR delta ;
removed after completion of
.BR delta .
.TP
x-file
Created during the execution of
.BR delta ;
renamed to SCCS file after completion of
.BR delta .
.TP
z-file
Created during the execution of
.BR delta ;
removed during the execution of
.BR delta .
.TP
/bin/diff
Program to compute differences
between the "gotten" file and the g-file .
.SH WARNINGS
Lines beginning with an \fBSOH\fR ASCII character (binary 001)
cannot be placed in the SCCS file unless the
.B SOH
is escaped.
This character has special meaning to SCCS
(see
.B sccsfile\c
(5)) and will cause an error.
.PP
A
.B get
of many SCCS files,
followed by a
.B delta
of those files, should be avoided when the
.B get
generates a large amount of data.
Instead,
multiple
.B "get/delta"
sequences should be used.
.PP
If the standard input
(\fB\-\fR)
is specified on the
.B delta
command line,
the
.B \-m
(if necessary)
and
.B \-y
keyletters
.I must
also be present.
Omission of these keyletters causes an error to occur.
.SH SEE ALSO
admin(1),
bdiff(1),
get(1),
help(1),
prs(1),
sccsfile(5).
.br
.I "Source Code Control System User's Guide" 
in the ZEUS Utilities Manual
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Use
.BR help (1)
for explanations.
